Conditions required for color matching in a color reproduction system are defined by:

$$\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{C} &= \mathbf{R} \mathbf{C}_{\mbox{eye}} 
\end{aligned}
$$

$$\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{M} &= (\mathbf{C P})^{-1},
\end{aligned}
$$

where $\mathbf{C}_{\mbox{eye}}$ are the human photoreceptor sensitivities and $\mathbf{R}$ is a full-rank $3 \times 3$ matrix.

From a light source, a dense array of light rays emerges in all directions. Before these light rays reach the eye, the light interacts with the objects in the world. If a light ray is not directed toward the eye, it strikes some surface and, as a result, a new set of rays is emitted in many directions. This process continues, producing more interactions and filling the space. In the middle of the space, an observer senses only a subset of the light rays that strike the eye. As a result of this complex pattern of interactions, even a single ray will form a complex image in the eye of the observer.

@fig-lightRay shows some pictures taken when illuminating a complex scene with a laser pointer that illuminate the scene with a narrow beam of light (this is the best approximation to the image produced by a single light ray that we could do at home).

![(a) Scene illuminated with a ceiling lamp. (b-c) Two images obtained by illuminating a scene with a laser pointer (the red line indicates the direction of the ray).](figures/taxonomy/lightGray2.png){#fig-lightRay}

The resulting images show the complexity of the interactions between the different surfaces. The sum of the contribution of all the light rays emitted by the light source will give rise to a natural looking picture. On each image, the approximate direction of the light beam produced by pointer is indicated by the red arrow in @fig-lightRay (b-c).

From the image gradient, we can extract the edge strength $e(x,y)$, which is defined as the gradient magnitude:

$$\begin{aligned}
    e(x,y) &= \lVert \nabla \ell(x,y) \rVert   & \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad \texttt{edge strength}
\end{aligned}$$ 

The edge strength quantifies the intensity of the variation at pixel $(x,y)$.

From the image gradient, we can extract the edge orientation $\theta(x,y)$:

$$\begin{aligned}
    \theta(x,y) &= \angle \nabla \ell =  \arctan \left( \frac{\partial \ell / \partial y}{\partial \ell / \partial x} \right) & \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad \texttt{edge orientation}
\end{aligned}$$ 

The edge orientation is perpendicular to the gradient direction.

The unit norm vector perpendicular to an edge is:

$$\begin{aligned}
{\bf n} = \frac{\nabla \ell}{\lVert \nabla \ell \rVert}
\end{aligned}$$

In a 3D vertical edge, using the projection equations, the derivative of $Y$ along the edge will be 

$$\begin{aligned}
\partial Y / \partial y &= 1/ \cos(\theta)\end{aligned}
$$

All the different constraints described previously can be written as an overdetermined system of linear equations. Each equation has the form:

$$\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{a}_i \mathbf{Y} = b_i
\end{aligned}$$

where $\mathbf{Y}$ is a vectorized version of the image $Y$. For example, if a pixel lies inside a planar face, it will generate three equations based on the planarity constraint, such as $\mathbf{a}_i = [0, \dots, 0, -1, 2, -1, 0, \dots, 0]$ and $b_i=0$.

The system can be solved by minimizing the cost function $J = \sum_i w_i (\mathbf{a}_i \mathbf{Y} - b_i)^2$, where $w_i$ are optional weights for different constraints. In matrix form, this is $\mathbf{A} \mathbf{Y} = \mathbf{b}$. Because the system is overdetermined, the solution is computed using the pseudoinverse:

$$\begin{aligned}
\bf Y = (\mathbf{A}^T \mathbf{A})^{-1} \mathbf{A}^T \mathbf{b}
\end{aligned}$$

This can be solved efficiently because the matrix $\mathbf{A}$ is very sparse.

Empirical Risk Minimization (ERM) is a formalization of learning from examples. This framework applies specifically to the supervised setting where we are learning a function that predicts $\mathbf{y}$ from $\mathbf{x}$ given many training examples $\{\mathbf{x}^{(i)},\mathbf{y}^{(i)}\}^N_{i=1}$. The idea is to minimize the average error (i.e., risk) we incur over all the training data (i.e., empirical distribution). The ERM problem is stated as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}
    \mathop{\mathrm{arg\,min}}_{f \in \mathcal{F}} \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^N \mathcal{L}(f(\mathbf{x}^{(i)}),\mathbf{y}^{(i)}) \quad\triangleleft\quad \text{ERM}
\end{aligned}
$$

Here, $\mathcal{F}$ is the hypothesis space, $\mathcal{L}$ is the loss function, and $\{\mathbf{x}^{(i)}, \mathbf{y}^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^N$ is the training data (example {`input`, `output`} pairs), and $f$ is the learned function.

Depending on the loss function, there is often an interpretation of ERM as doing maximum likelihood probabilistic inference. In this interpretation, we are trying to infer the hypothesis $f$ that assigns the highest probability to the data. For a model that predicts $\mathbf{y}$ given $\mathbf{x}$, the max likelihood $f$ is:

$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathop{\mathrm{arg\,max}}_f p\big(\{\mathbf{y}^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^N \bigm | \{\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^N, f\big) \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad\text{Max likelihood learning}
\end{aligned}
$$

The term $p\big(\{\mathbf{y}^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^N \bigm | \{\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^N, f\big)$ is called the likelihood of the $\mathbf{y}$ values given the model $f$ and the observed $\mathbf{x}$ values, and maximizing this quantity is called maximum likelihood learning.

Priors $p(f)$ can be used for inferring the most probable hypothesis. When a prior is used in conjunction with a likelihood function, we arrive at maximum a posteriori learning (MAP learning), which infers the most probable hypothesis given the training data:

$$\begin{aligned}
    &\mathop{\mathrm{arg\,max}}_f p\big(f \bigm | \{\mathbf{x}^{(i)}, \mathbf{y}^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^N\big) \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad \text{MAP learning}\\
    & = \mathop{\mathrm{arg\,max}}_f p\big(\{\mathbf{y}^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^N \bigm | \{\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^N, f\big)p\big(f\big) \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad \text{by Bayes' rule}
\end{aligned}$$

The 0-1 loss is a loss function used to minimize misclassifications. It is defined as:

$$
\begin{aligned}
    \mathcal{L}(\hat{\mathbf{y}},\mathbf{y}) = \mathbb{1}(\hat{\mathbf{y}}\neq\mathbf{y}),
\end{aligned}
$$ where $\mathbb{1}$ is the indicator function that evaluates to 1 if and only if its argument is true, and 0 otherwise.

Because minimizing 0-1 loss is NP-hard, people commonly use cross-entropy loss, which is continuous and differentiable (making it easier to optimize):
$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathcal{L}(\hat{\mathbf{y}},\mathbf{y}) = H(\mathbf{y}, \hat{\mathbf{y}}) = - \sum_{k=1}^K y_k \log \hat{y}_k \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad \text{cross-entropy loss}
\end{aligned}$$ 

The way to think about this is $\hat{y}_k$ should represent the probability we think the image is an image of class $k$. Under that interpretation, minimizing cross-entropy maximizes the log likelihood of the ground truth observation $\mathbf{y}$ under our model's prediction $\hat{\mathbf{y}}$.

Regularization refers to mechanisms that penalize function complexity so that we avoid learning too flexible a function that overfits. Typically, regularizers are terms we add to the objective that prefer simple functions in the hypothesis space, all else being equal. They therefore embody the principle of Occam's razor.

The general form of a regularized objective is:
$$\begin{aligned}
    J(\theta) = \overbrace{\frac{1}{N} \sum^N_{i=1} \mathcal{L}(f_{\theta}(x)^{(i)}, y^{(i)})}^\text{data fit loss} + \underbrace{\lambda R(\theta)}_\text{regularizer}
\end{aligned}$$
where $\lambda$ is a hyperparameter that controls the strength of the regularization.

The $\mathbb{1}$ is the indicator function, which is defined as:
$$\begin{equation*}
    \mathbb{1}(x) = \begin{cases}
                1 & \text{if $x$ is true} \\
                0 & \text{otherwise}
             \end{cases}
\end{equation*}$$

Linear layers are the workhorses of deep nets. Almost all parameters of the network are contained in these layers; we call these parameters the weights and biases. They are defined by the transformation:

$$
\begin{aligned}
    \textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{\mathbf{x}_{\texttt{out}}} = \textcolor[rgb]{0.2, 0.6, 1.0}{\mathbf{W}} \textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{\mathbf{x}_{\texttt{in}}} + \textcolor[rgb]{0.2, 0.6, 1.0}{\mathbf{b}} & \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad\texttt{linear}
\end{aligned}
$$

If a net only contained linear layers then it could only compute linear functions, because the composition of $N$ linear functions is a linear function. Activation layers add nonlinearity. Activation layers are typically pointwise functions, applying a scalar to scalar mapping on each dimension of the input vector. Typically parameters of these layers, if any, are not learned (but they can be). Some common activation layers include:

(see figure: Common pointwise nonlinearities.)

$$
\begin{aligned}
    \textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{x_{\texttt{out}}}[i] &= 
        \begin{cases}
            1, &\text{if} \quad \textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{x_{\texttt{in}}}[i] > 0\\
            0,              & \text{otherwise}
        \end{cases} & \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad \texttt{threshold}\\
    \textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{x_{\texttt{out}}}[i] &= \frac{1}{1 + e^{-\textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{x_{\texttt{in}}}[i]}} & \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad \texttt{sigmoid}\\
    \textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{x_{\texttt{out}}}[i] &= 2*\texttt{sigmoid}(2*\textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{x_{\texttt{in}}}[i])-1 & \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad \texttt{tanh}\\
    \textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{x_{\texttt{out}}}[i] &= \max(\textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{x_{\texttt{in}}}[i],0) & \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad \texttt{relu}\\
    \textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{x_{\texttt{out}}}[i] &= 
        \begin{cases}
            \max(\textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{x_{\texttt{in}}}[i],0), &\text{if} \quad \textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{x_{\texttt{in}}}[i] \geq 0\\
            \textcolor[rgb]{0.2, 0.6, 1.0}{\alpha}\min(\textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{x_{\texttt{in}}}[i],0),              & \text{otherwise}
        \end{cases} & \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad \texttt{leaky-relu}
\end{aligned}
$$

Leaky ReLU is an activation function defined as:

$$\textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{x_{\texttt{out}}}[i] = 
        \begin{cases}
            \max(\textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{x_{\texttt{in}}}[i],0), &\text{if} \quad \textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{x_{\texttt{in}}}[i] \geq 0\\
            \textcolor[rgb]{0.2, 0.6, 1.0}{\alpha}\min(\textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{x_{\texttt{in}}}[i],0),              & \text{otherwise}
        \end{cases} \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad \texttt{leaky-relu}$$

Batchnorm standardizes each neural activation with respect to its mean and variance over a batch of datapoints. Mathematically:

$$
\begin{aligned}
    \textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{x_{\texttt{out}}}[i] = 
    \textcolor[rgb]{0.2, 0.6, 1.0}{\gamma}
    \frac{\textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{x_{\texttt{in}}}[i] - \mathbb{E}[\textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{x_{\texttt{in}}}[i]]}
         {\sqrt{\texttt{Var}[\textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{x_{\texttt{in}}}[i]]}} 
    + \textcolor[rgb]{0.2, 0.6, 1.0}{\beta} & \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad \texttt{batchnorm}
\end{aligned}
$$

where $\textcolor[rgb]{0.2, 0.6, 1.0}{\gamma}$ and $\textcolor[rgb]{0.2, 0.6, 1.0}{\beta}$ are learned parameters that maintain expressivity, allowing the layer to output values with non-zero mean and non-unit variance. Most commonly, batchnorm uses training batch statistics. At test time, aggregate statistics from the training data are typically used, though test batch statistics can be used to achieve invariance to changes in data statistics.

The local gradient arrays provide a simple formula for computing the gradient $\frac{\partial J}{\partial \theta}$ required to update $\theta$ and minimize the cost:

$$\begin{aligned}
    \frac{\partial J}{\partial \theta} &= \underbrace{\frac{\partial J}{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\texttt{out}}}}_{\mathbf{g}_{\texttt{out}}} \underbrace{\frac{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\texttt{out}}}{\partial \theta}}_{\mathbf{L}^{\theta}} = \mathbf{g}_{\texttt{out}}\mathbf{L}^{\theta}\\
    \theta^{i+1} &\leftarrow \theta^{i} - \eta \Big(\frac{\partial J}{\partial \theta}\Big)^\mathsf{T}&&\quad\quad \triangleleft \quad \texttt{update}
\end{aligned}$$

By convention, $\theta$ is a column vector while $\frac{\partial J}{\partial \theta}$ is a row vector, which necessitates the transpose in the update step.

While backpropagation is often described for a single datapoint, the total cost function typically represents the average of losses over all datapoints in a training set, $\{\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^N$. Because the gradient of a sum is the sum of the gradients, we can compute the gradient for the whole dataset using the identity:

$$\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial \frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^N J_i(\theta)}{\partial \theta} = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^N \frac{\partial J_i(\theta)}{\partial \theta}
\end{aligned}$$

To compute a gradient update for algorithms like stochastic gradient descent, we apply backpropagation in batch mode by running it over each datapoint in the batch (often in parallel) and then averaging the results.

The gradient operation is linear with respect to summation. Specifically, for a cost function defined as an average of individual losses:

$$\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial \frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^N J_i(\theta)}{\partial \theta} = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^N \frac{\partial J_i(\theta)}{\partial \theta}
\end{aligned}$$

This principle ensures that the gradient of a sum of terms is the sum of the gradients of each term.

In the continuous domain, there is a direct relationship between the Fourier transform of a function and the Fourier transform of its derivative:
$$
\frac{\partial \ell (x)}{\partial x}  
\xrightarrow{\mathscr{F}} 
j w \mathscr{L} (w)
$$

When working with two-dimensional (2D) images, partial derivatives can be approximated along the $n$ and $m$ components using kernels such as:
$$
\begin{bmatrix}
  1 \\
  -1
\end{bmatrix}
~~~~~~~
\begin{bmatrix}
  1 & -1
\end{bmatrix}
$$
A limitation of this discretization is that filters of length 2 shift the output image by half a pixel, leading to spatial misalignment. This can be addressed by using alternative operators like the Roberts cross, which uses a rotated reference frame.

The Roberts cross operator is a 2D derivative discretization that removes spatial misalignment by using a rotated reference frame. It was introduced in 1963 and is defined by the following kernels:
$$
\begin{bmatrix}
  1 & ~0\\
  0 & -1
\end{bmatrix}
~~~~~~~
\begin{bmatrix}
  ~0 & 1 \\
  -1 & 0
\end{bmatrix}
$$

The **inverse Fourier transform** relation is defined as: 

$$\ell\left[n,m ight] = \mathcal{F}^{-1} \left\{ \mathscr{L}\left[u,v ight] ight\} =  rac{1}{NM} \sum_{u=0}^{N-1} \sum_{v=0}^{M-1} \mathscr{L}\left[u,v ight] \exp{ \left(+2\pi j \left( rac{u\, n}{N} +  rac{v\, m}{M} ight) ight) } $${#eq-inversefourier}

As seen from the inverse transform equation, the image is rewritten as a sum of complex exponentials rather than a sum of offset pixel values. Each exponential is at a different frequency, called a spatial frequency for images because they describe how quickly things vary across space. To construct an image from a Fourier transform $\mathscr{L}\left[u,v ight]$, we add in the corresponding amount of that particular complex exponential (conjugated).

Using the fact that $e_{N-u, M-v} = e_{-u,-v}$, another equivalent way to write the Fourier transform is to sum over the frequency intervals $[-N/2, N/2]$ and $[-M/2, M/2]$. This is especially useful for the inverse transform, which can be written as: 

$$\ell\left[n,m ight] =  rac{1}{NM} \sum_{u=-N/2}^{N/2} \sum_{v=-M/2}^{M/2} \mathscr{L}\left[u,v ight]\exp{ \left(+2 rac{\pi j (u\, n)}{N} +  rac{v\, m}{M} ight) } $${#eq-inversefourier2}

This formulation allows us to arrange the coefficients in the complex plane so that the zero frequency, or DC, coefficient is at the center. Slow, large variations correspond to complex exponentials of frequencies near the origin. If the amplitudes of the complex conjugate exponentials are the same, then their sum will represent a cosine wave; if their amplitudes are opposite, it will be a sine wave. Frequencies further away from the origin represent faster variation with movement across space.

To apply a convolutional layer to a multichannel image, such as an RGB image $\mathbf{x}_{\texttt{in}}\in \mathbb{R}^{3 \times N \times M}$, we use a multichannel filter $\mathbf{w} \in \mathbb{R}^{C \times K \times K}$. We filter each input channel with the corresponding filter channel and then sum the responses:
$$\begin{aligned}    \mathbf{x}_{\texttt{out}}= \sum_{c} \mathbf{w}[c,:,:] \star \mathbf{x}_{\texttt{in}}[c,:,:] + b[c] & \quad \triangleleft \quad\texttt{conv}\quad \text{(multichannel in)}
\end{aligned}$$

$$\begin{aligned}x_{\texttt{out}}[n,m] =
b + \sum_{k_1,k_2=-K}^K w[k_1,k_2] x_{\texttt{in}}[s_n n-k_1,s_m m-k_2] & \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad \texttt{conv}\quad \text{(strided)}
\end{aligned}$$

Dilated convolution is similar to strided convolution but spaces out the filter itself rather than spacing out where the filter is applied to the image:

$$\begin{aligned}
x_{\texttt{out}}[n,m] =
b + \sum_{k_1,k_2=-K}^K w[k_1,k_2] x_{\texttt{in}}[n-d_kk_1,m-d_kk_2] & \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad \texttt{conv}\quad \text{(dilated)}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$\begin{aligned}
x_{\texttt{out}}[n,m] =
b + \sum_{k_1,k_2=-K}^K w[k_1,k_2] x_{\texttt{in}}[n-d_kk_1,m-d_kk_2] & \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad \texttt{conv}\quad \text{(dilated)}
\end{aligned}
$$

A recurrent layer is defined by the following equations: 
$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{h}_t &= f(\mathbf{h}_{t-1}, \mathbf{x}_{\texttt{in}}[t]) &\triangleleft \quad\quad \text{update state based on input and previous state}\\
    \mathbf{x}_{\texttt{out}}[t]&= g(\mathbf{h}_t) &\triangleleft \quad\quad \text{produce output based on state}
\end{aligned}$$

This is a layer that includes a state variable, $\mathbf{h}$. The operation of the layer depends on its state. The layer also updates its state every time it is called; therefore, the state acts as a kind of memory.

(see figure: Backpropagation through time in an RNN with a loss at each timestep.)

Running an RNN for many timesteps can result in very large computation graphs. The gradient computation involves a series of matrix multiplies that is $\mathcal{O}(T)$ in length. For example, computing the gradient $\frac{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\texttt{out}}[t]}{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\texttt{in}}[0]}$ involves the following product:

$$\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\texttt{out}}[t]}{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\texttt{in}}[0]} = \frac{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\texttt{out}}[t]}{\partial \mathbf{h}_T} \frac{\partial \mathbf{h}_T}{\partial \mathbf{h}_{T-1}} \cdots \frac{\partial \mathbf{h}_1}{\partial \mathbf{h}_0} \frac{\partial \mathbf{h}_0}{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\texttt{in}}[0]}
\end{aligned}$$

In an RNN with ReLU nonlinearities, if all ReLUs are active, the gradient involves a product of $T$ weight matrices $\mathbf{W}$. If values in $\mathbf{W}$ are large, the gradient will explode; if they are small, the gradient will vanish. This makes the system numerically unstable. One solution is to use specialized RNN units, such as LSTMs, that have better numerical properties.

One component picks the indices of the cell state to delete:
$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{f}_t &= \sigma(\mathbf{W}_f\mathbf{h}_{t-1} + \mathbf{U}_f\mathbf{x}_{\texttt{in}}[t] + \mathbf{b}_f) &\triangleleft \quad\quad\text{decide which indices to forget}
\end{aligned}$$
where $\mathbf{f}_t$ is a vector of length equal to the length of the cell state $\mathbf{c}_t$, and $\sigma$ is the sigmoid function that outputs values in the range $[0,1]$. The idea is that most values in $\mathbf{f}_t$ will be near either $1$ (remember) or 0 (forget). Later $\mathbf{f}_t$ will be pointwise multiplied by the cell state to forget the values where $\mathbf{f}_t$ is zero.

Another component chooses what values to write to the cell state, and where to write them:
$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{i}_t &= \sigma(\mathbf{W}_i\mathbf{h}_{t-1} + \mathbf{U}_i\mathbf{x}_{\texttt{in}}[t] + \mathbf{b}_i) &\triangleleft \quad\quad\text{which indices to write to}\\
    \tilde{\mathbf{c}}_t &= \texttt{tanh}(\mathbf{W}_C\mathbf{h}_{t-1} + \mathbf{U}_c\mathbf{x}_{\texttt{in}}[t] + \mathbf{b}_c) &\triangleleft \quad\quad\text{what to write to those indices}
\end{aligned}$$
where $\mathbf{i}_t$ is similar to $\mathbf{f}_t$ (it is a vector whose values are nearly 0 or 1 of length equal to the length of the cell state) and it determines which indices of $\tilde{\mathbf{c}}_t$ will be written to the cell state.

We use the forget and input vectors to actually update the cell state:
$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{c}_t &= \mathbf{f}_t \odot \mathbf{c}_{t-1} + \mathbf{i}_t \odot \tilde{\mathbf{c}}_{t} &\triangleleft \quad\quad\text{update the cell state}
\end{aligned}$$

Finally, given the updated cell state, we read from it and use the values to determine the hidden state to output:
$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{o}_t &= \sigma(\mathbf{W}_o\mathbf{h}_{t-1} + \mathbf{U}_o\mathbf{x}_{\texttt{in}}[t] + \mathbf{b}_o) &\triangleleft \quad\quad\text{which indices of cell state to use}\\
    \mathbf{h}_t &= \mathbf{o}_t \odot \texttt{tanh}(\mathbf{c}_t) &\triangleleft \quad\quad\text{use these to determine next hidden state}
\end{aligned}$$
The basic idea is it should be easy, by default, not to output everything in the cell state.

A fully connected layer (fc layer) over tokens is defined as a mapping from $N_1$ input tokens to $N_2$ output tokens. It is parameterized by a matrix $\mathbf{W} \in \mathbb{R}^{N_2 \times N_1}$ and optionally by a set of token biases $\mathbf{b} \in \mathbb{R}^{N_2 \times d}$:

$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{T}_{\texttt{out}}&= \mathbf{W}\mathbf{T}_{\texttt{in}}+ \mathbf{b} \quad\quad &\triangleleft \quad \text{fc layer over tokens}
\end{aligned}$$

To model nonlinear functions, token nets use tokenwise operators, which apply a transformation to each token individually and independently. Given a nonlinear function $F_{\theta}: \mathbb{R}^N \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^N$, a tokenwise nonlinearity layer is expressed as:

$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{T}_{\texttt{out}}= 
        \begin{bmatrix}
        F_{\theta}(\mathbf{T}_{\texttt{in}}[0,:]) \\
        \vdots \\
        F_{\theta}(\mathbf{T}_{\texttt{in}}[N-1,:]) \\
        \end{bmatrix}\quad\quad \triangleleft \quad\text{per-token nonlinearity}
\end{aligned}$$

This generalizes pointwise nonlinearities in regular neural nets (like ReLU). A key difference is that while standard ReLUs have no learnable parameters, $F_{\theta}$ in token nets is often a multilayer perceptron (MLP) with its own learnable weights and biases, making these pointwise operations highly expressive.

We normalize the vector $\mathbf{s}$ using the softmax function to give us our attention weights $\mathbf{a} \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times 1}$. Finally, rather than applying $\mathbf{a}$ over token codes directly (i.e., taking a weighted sum over tokens), we take a weighted sum over token value vectors to obtain $\mathbf{T}_{\texttt{out}}$:

$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{a} &= \texttt{softmax}(\mathbf{s})\\
    \mathbf{T}_{\texttt{out}}&= \begin{bmatrix}
        a_1\mathbf{v}_1^\mathsf{T}\\
        \vdots \\
        a_N\mathbf{v}_N^\mathsf{T}\\
    \end{bmatrix} 
\end{aligned}$$

To compute the query, key, and value for a set of input tokens, $\mathbf{T}_{\texttt{in}}$, we apply the same linear transformations to each token in the set, resulting in matrices $\mathbf{Q}_{\texttt{in}}, \mathbf{K}_{\texttt{in}} \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times m}$ and $\mathbf{V}_{\texttt{in}} \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times d}$, where each row is the query/key/value for each token. Note that the query and key vectors must have the same dimensionality, $m$, because we take a dot product between them. Conversely, the value vectors must match the dimensionality of the token code vectors, $d$, because these are summed up to produce the new token code vectors.

$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{Q}_{\texttt{in}}&= \mathbf{T}_{\texttt{in}}\mathbf{W}_q^\mathsf{T}&\triangleleft \quad\quad \text{query matrix} \\
    \mathbf{K}_{\texttt{in}}&= \mathbf{T}_{\texttt{in}}\mathbf{W}_k^\mathsf{T}&\triangleleft \quad\quad \text{key matrix}\\
    \mathbf{V}_{\texttt{in}}&= \mathbf{T}_{\texttt{in}}\mathbf{W}_v^\mathsf{T}&\triangleleft \quad\quad \text{value matrix}
\end{aligned}$$

Finally, we have the attention equation:

$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{A} &= f(\mathbf{T}_{\texttt{in}}) = \texttt{softmax}\Big(\frac{\mathbf{Q}_{\texttt{in}}\mathbf{K}_{\texttt{in}}^\mathsf{T}}{\sqrt{m}}\Big) &\triangleleft \quad\quad \text{attention matrix}\\
    \mathbf{T}_{\texttt{out}}&= \mathbf{A}\mathbf{V}_{\texttt{in}}
\end{aligned}$$

where the softmax is taken within each row. In expanded detail, here are the full mechanics of a self-attention layer (@fig-transformers-attn_arch):

(see figure: Self-attention layer expanded. The nodes with the dashed outline correspond to each other; they represent one query being matched against one key to result in a scalar similarity value, in the gray...)

To find the missing value $a$ that maximizes the probability of a 1D image under the wavelet marginal model, we use a single filter kernel $[-1, 1]$. Applying this filter to a signal with a missing value results in: $$\boldsymbol\ell_0 = \boldsymbol\ell\circ \left[-1, 1\right] = \left[0,~0,~ 0,~0,~ 0,~ 1-a,~ a,~ 0,~ 0,~ 0,~ 0\right]$$ The probability of the signal as a function of $a$ is: $$p(\boldsymbol\ell\bigm | a) = \frac{1}{(2s/r \Gamma(1/r))^N} \exp - \frac{\left| 1-a \right|^r+\left| a \right|^r} {s^r}$$ For $r > 1$, the optimal value is $a=0.5$, producing a smooth signal (equivalent to a Gaussian prior). For $r=1$, any $a \in [0,1]$ is equally probable. For $r < 1$, the maxima occur at $a=0$ or $a=1$, meaning the model prefers sharp step edges over smooth transitions.

![Best values of $a$, as a function of $r$, that maximize the probability of the 1D image $\left[1,1,1,1,1,a,0,0,0,0,0 \right]$ under the wavelet prior model. The optimal $a$ changes when crossing the $r=1$ value.](figures/statistical_image_models/best_a_s1.png){width="60%" #fig-surface_best_a}

(see figure: Reconstructed signal using (left) $r=2$ and (right) $r=0.5$. The estimated values of $a$ are 0.5 and 1.)

A token-wise MLP applies the same MLP $F_{\theta}$ to each token in a sequence. The $F_{\theta}$ consists of linear layers and pointwise nonlinearities. Assuming no biases, the linear layers in $F_{\theta}$ have the form:
$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{t}_{\texttt{out}} &= \mathbf{W}\mathbf{t}_{\texttt{in}}
\end{aligned}$$
When applied to each token in the sequence, we have:
$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{T}_{\texttt{out}}&= \mathbf{T}_{\texttt{in}}\mathbf{W}^{\mathsf{T}}
\end{aligned}$$
This is identical to the query operation, meaning the linear layers of the token-wise MLP can all be written as convolutions with kernel size 1. Since the pointwise nonlinearities in the MLP are applied neuronwise, they function identically to the pointwise nonlinearity in CNNs. Therefore, a token-wise MLP is equivalent to a series of convolutions interleaved with neuronwise-nonlinearities, which is a CNN.

The likelihood term is approximated by sampling. For each datapoint $x$, we take a single sample from this expectation. First, we encode $x$ to parameterize $q_{\psi}(Z \mid x)$. Then we sample a $z$ from $q_{\psi}(Z \mid x)$. Finally, we decode this $z$ to parameterize $p_{\theta}(X \mid z)$ and measure the probability this distribution places on our observed input $x$:

$$\begin{aligned}
    \mu_z, \sigma^2_z &= f_{\psi}(x) & \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad\text{Encode $x$}\\
    z &\sim \mathcal{N}(\mu_z, \sigma^2_z) 
    & \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad\text{Sample $z$}
\end{aligned}$$

$$\begin{aligned}  
    \mu_x, \sigma^2_x &= g_{\theta}(z) & \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad\text{Decode $z$}&\\
    \log p_{\theta}(x | z) &= \log \mathcal{N}(x; \mu_x, \sigma^2_x) &\\
                          &= \log\frac{1}{\sigma_x\sqrt{2\pi}} - \frac{(x - \mu_x)^2}{2\sigma_x^2} & \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad\text{Measure likelihood}
\end{aligned}$$

In a VAE, we encode an input, then decode it, and finally measure the reconstruction error between our original input and the output of the decoder. This process is analogous to a standard autoencoder. The relationship is shown in the likelihood calculation:

$$\begin{aligned}  
    \mu_x, \sigma^2_x &= g_{\theta}(z) & \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad\text{Decode $z$}&\\
    \log p_{\theta}(x | z) &= \log \mathcal{N}(x; \mu_x, \sigma^2_x) &\\
                          &= \log\frac{1}{\sigma_x\sqrt{2\pi}} - \frac{\overbrace{(x - \mu_x)^2}^{\text{reconstruction error}}}{2\sigma_x^2} & \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad\text{Measure likelihood}
\end{aligned}$$

One way to express the probability of data $\mathbf{x}$ is as the marginal likelihood, marginalizing over all unobserved latent variables $\mathbf{z}$:
$$\begin{aligned}
    p_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}) = \int_{\mathbf{z}} p_{\theta}(\mathbf{x} \bigm | \mathbf{z})p_{\mathbf{z}}(\mathbf{z})d\mathbf{z}
\end{aligned}$$

While standard Gaussian mixture models use a finite number of components, VAEs use an infinite mixture of Gaussians ($k \rightarrow \infty$). To parameterize an infinite mixture without learning infinite parameters, we make the mean and variance be functions of an underlying continuous variable. The function the VAE uses is $g_{\theta}$, decomposed into $g_{\theta}^{\mu}$ and $g_{\theta}^{\Sigma}$ to separately model the means and variances. Using a unit Gaussian distribution as the continuous domain to integrate over, the infinite mixture is described as:
$$\begin{aligned}
    p_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}) = \int_{\mathbf{z}} \underbrace{\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{x}; g^{\mu}_{\theta}(\mathbf{z}), g^{\Sigma}_{\theta}(\mathbf{z}))}_{p_{\theta}(\mathbf{x} \| \mathbf{z})}\underbrace{\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{z}; \mathbf{0}, \mathbf{I})}_{p_{\mathbf{z}}(\mathbf{z})}d\mathbf{z}
\end{aligned}$$
This models an infinite mixture as an integral that marginalizes over a continuous latent variable $\mathbf{z}$.

The difficulty of optimizing VAEs lies in the fact that the objective is intractable, requiring integration over an infinite set of deep net forward passes. Once an approximation is derived, optimization can be performed via backpropagation. With the objective and hypothesis given, the VAE learning problem is defined as:
$$\begin{aligned}
    \theta^* &= \mathop{\mathrm{arg\,max}}_{\theta} L(\{\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^N, \theta)\\
    &= \mathop{\mathrm{arg\,max}}_{\theta} \sum_{i=1}^N \log \underbrace{\int_{\mathbf{z}} \overbrace{\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{x}^{(i)}; g^{\mu}_{\theta}(\mathbf{z}), g^{\Sigma}_{\theta}(\mathbf{z}))}^{p_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{(i)} \| \mathbf{z})} \overbrace{\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{z}; \mathbf{0}, \mathbf{I})}^{p_{\mathbf{z}}(\mathbf{z})}d\mathbf{z}}_{p_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{(i)})}
\end{aligned}$$

Homogeneous coordinates allow using transformations as matrix multiplications. We can build complex transformations by combining these four transformations:

$$\mathbf{p}' = 
    \begin{bmatrix}
    1 & q_x & 0 \\
    q_y & 1 & 0 \\
    0 & 0 & 1
    \end{bmatrix}
    \begin{bmatrix}
    s_x & 0 & 0 \\
    0 & s_y & 0 \\
    0 & 0 & 1
    \end{bmatrix}
    \begin{bmatrix}
    \cos(\theta) & \sin(\theta) & 0 \\
    -\sin(\theta) & \cos(\theta) & 0 \\
    0 & 0 & 1
    \end{bmatrix}
    \begin{bmatrix}
    1 & 0 & t_x \\
    0 & 1 & t_y \\
    0 & 0 & 1
    \end{bmatrix}
    \mathbf{p}$$

(see figure: Summary of 2D geometric transformations and their formulation in homogeneous coordinates.)

As matrix multiplication is noncommutative, the order in which operations are performed is important. All of the geometric transformations described are relative to the origin. To rotate an image around an arbitrary central location, you must first translate to put that location at the origin, then rotate, and then translate back:

$$\mathbf{p}' = 
    \begin{bmatrix}
    1 & 0 & t_x \\
    0 & 1 & t_y \\
    0 & 0 & 1
    \end{bmatrix}
    \begin{bmatrix}
    \cos(\theta) & \sin(\theta) & 0 \\
    -\sin(\theta) & \cos(\theta) & 0 \\
    0 & 0 & 1
    \end{bmatrix}
    \begin{bmatrix}
    1 & 0 & -t_x \\
    0 & 1 & -t_y \\
    0 & 0 & 1
    \end{bmatrix}
    \mathbf{p}$$

To recover the world coordinates for every pixel, we assume a pinhole camera model. Using the depth $z[n,m]$ and the intrinsic camera parameters $\mathbf{K}$, we obtain the 3D coordinates for a pixel with image coordinates $[n,m]$ in the camera reference frame as:

$$\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}
X \\
Y \\
Z 
\end{bmatrix}
= z[n,m] \times \mathbf{K}^{-1} \times
\begin{bmatrix}
n \\
m \\
1 
\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}$$

Note that $\mathbf{K}$ are the intrinsic camera parameters for the reference image.

A radiance field is a representation of part of the plenoptic function, based on the idea that the optical content of a scene can be modeled as a cloud of colorful particles with different levels of transparency. Such a representation can be rendered into images using volume rendering. We define a radiance field $L$ as a mapping from the coordinates in a scene to color and density (i.e., transparency) values of the scene content at those coordinates. The coordinates can be 2D ($X,Y$) or 3D ($X,Y,Z$) and may also contain angular dimensions ($\psi$ and $\phi$) representing the viewing angle. Representing color using $r,g,b$ values and density with a scalar $\sigma$, a radiance field is a function:

$$\begin{aligned}
    L: X,Y,Z,\psi,\phi \rightarrow r,g,b,\sigma \quad\quad\triangleleft \quad\text{radiance field}
\end{aligned}$$

The angular coordinates allow an object's color to vary depending on the viewing angle, which is essential for shiny surfaces. We use $L^c$ and $L^\sigma$ to denote the subcomponents that output color and density respectively:
$$\begin{aligned}
    L^c&: X,Y,Z,\psi,\phi \rightarrow r,g,b\\
    L^{\sigma}&: X,Y,Z,\psi,\phi \rightarrow\sigma
\end{aligned}$$

To recover the world coordinates for every pixel, we assume a pinhole camera model. Using the depth $z[n,m]$ and the camera matrix $\mathbf{K}$, we obtain the 3D coordinates for a pixel with image coordinates $(n,m)$ in the camera reference frame as:

$$\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}
X \\
Y \\ 
Z 
\end{bmatrix}
= z[n,m] \times \mathbf{K}^{-1} \times
\begin{bmatrix}
n \\
m \\ 
1 
\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}$$

The intrinsic camera parameters (using perspective projection) are:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{M}
= 
\begin{bmatrix}
a & 0 & n_0\\
0 & a & m_0 \\ 
0 & 0 & 1 
\end{bmatrix}.
\end{aligned}
$$

[FIGURE:2c6cb957a032955d | A photograph of a camera on a tripod viewing a set of colored blocks on a surface. Two 3D coordinate systems are overlaid: a world coordinate system $(X, Y, Z)$ centered on the blocks, and a camera...]

The extrinsic camera parameters will model the 3D rotation, which is mainly around the $X$-axis (we will ignore the translation of the origin):
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{R}
= 
\begin{bmatrix}
1 & 0 & 0\\
0 & \cos(\theta) & -\sin(\theta) \\ 
0 & \sin(\theta) & \cos(\theta) 
\end{bmatrix}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Here are the equations for a cross-attention layer:
$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{Q}_{t} = \mathbf{T}_{t}\mathbf{W}_{t,q}^\mathsf{T}\\
    \mathbf{K}_{\ell} = \mathbf{T}_{\ell}\mathbf{W}_{\ell,k}^\mathsf{T}\\
    \mathbf{V}_{\ell} = \mathbf{T}_{\ell}\mathbf{W}_{\ell,v}^\mathsf{T}\\
    \mathbf{A}_{\texttt{cross}} = \texttt{softmax}(\frac{\mathbf{Q}_{t}\mathbf{K}_{\ell}^\mathsf{T}}{\sqrt{m}})\\
    \mathbf{T}_{\texttt{out}, \texttt{cross}} = \mathbf{A}_{\texttt{cross}}\mathbf{V}_{\ell}
\end{aligned}$$

where variables subscripted with $t$ are associated with text tokens and variables subscripted with $\ell$ are associated with image tokens. Then, the tokens output by cross-attention can be combined with tokens output by self-attention by weighted summation:

$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{T} = w_{\texttt{cross}}\mathbf{T}_{\texttt{out}, \texttt{cross}}  + w_{\texttt{self}}\mathbf{T}_{\texttt{out}, \texttt{self}}
\end{aligned}$$

where $\mathbf{T}_{\texttt{out}, \texttt{self}}$ is regular self-attention on the input text token $\mathbf{T}_t$, and $w_{\texttt{cross}}$ and $w_{\texttt{self}}$ are optional learnable weights.

